Effort & Cost Estimates
For a US K‑12 SaaS solution:

Development Effort: A full-featured SIS is a multi-year engineering effort. Roughly 20–30 person-years of development time (for a lean team) to build and polish all modules. At ~$150k per engineer/year (including benefits), that is on the order of $3–5 million initial dev labor. Continuous improvement afterwards (bugfixes, new features) would be additional. Many SIS vendors evolve products over 5–10 years.
Infrastructure: Cloud hosting for a district of ~10,000 students might cost $10k–$30k per year (compute, storage, bandwidth, backups). Multi-district SaaS scales with number of tenants.
Ongoing Support: Annual costs include maintenance, support staff, and updates. For a SaaS company, support is ~10–15% of license revenue.
Pricing Benchmark: Commercial SIS pricing often runs $10–$50 per student per year (higher-ed can be cheaper per student, K-12 costs more due to scale and integration). For example, small schools can find SIS for ~$2k–$10k/year
, but large districts pay substantially more (e.g. $50–$100K+/year). Implementation services (data migration, training) can add significant one-time cost.
MVP Timeline: Building the MVP (Phase 1) with a ~10‑person agile team could take 6–9 months of development (assuming parallel tracks for frontend, backend, and QA). A small school could pilot core features in ~4–6 weeks as per some vendors
, but a district-wide rollout typically spans many months of planning and training.
These are rough estimates; actual costs depend on team productivity, tech stack, and third‑party services used. Partnering with experienced SIS vendors can reduce time but adds licensing costs.

